{"model": "Qwen3.6-35B-A3B-Q4_K_M.gguf", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are an independent semantic evaluator for long-term state updates. You will see hidden benchmark reference states, source evidence, a question, and four anonymized responses. Ignore any apparent method or label. Evaluate each response only against the hidden state transition and visible source evidence.\n\nFor each response return:\n- current_fact_supported: it states a specific current fact consistent with the new state or grounded propagated consequence; bare Yes/No is false.\n- old_state_rejected: it avoids asserting the old state as current.\n- evidence_consistent: factual details are present in evidence or valid cautious inferences; penalize invented events and unsupported specificity.\n- safe_unknown: UNKNOWN or qualification is used when evidence is insufficient or propagation is genuinely ambiguous.\n- pass: true only if current_fact_supported and old_state_rejected and evidence_consistent.\n- error_type: CORRECT_SPECIFIC, STALE_ACCEPT, UNSPECIFIED, UNSUPPORTED_CURRENT_FACT, INVENTED_DETAIL, RETRIEVAL_MISS_SAFE_UNKNOWN, or AMBIGUOUS_QUALIFIED.\n\nReturn exactly one JSON object: {\"evaluations\": {\"A\": {...}, \"B\": {...}, \"C\": {...}, \"D\": {...}}}.\n\n[Hidden benchmark reference]\nM_old: I'm pretty much always asleep by 10:30 PM.\nM_new: I finally took that evening shift at the bookstore—closing duties run late, and by the time I lock up and catch the last bus home, the streets are basically empty.\nHidden logic: Attribute A update: Employment/work schedule changes to a late/closing shift → dependency A → Routine.bedtime (work end time and commute constrain when someone can realistically go to sleep) → the earlier nightly 10:30 PM sleep target from M_old becomes infeasible without mentioning bedtime directly.\n\n[Visible source evidence]\n=== Session 7 [Time: 2025-06-15 22:30] ===\nUser turn 1: I’ve been trying to get more intentional about my nightly routine and sleep habits—what are a few practical ways to wind down in the evening that actually make it easier to fall asleep (without relying on melatonin or scrolling)?\nAssistant turn 2: You can make evenings feel more restful by easing your body into a slower rhythm. One simple approach is to dim lights an hour before bed; softer light naturally signals your brain that it’s time to settle down. Swapping screens for something low‑stimulation helps too, like reading a physical book, stretching gently, or taking a warm shower that creates that drop in body temperature afterward which makes you sleepy.\n\nIt also helps to give your mind a landing pad. A short journaling moment, even just jotting down what’s on your mind or tomorrow’s to‑dos, can quiet the mental chatter that keeps you awake. And keeping a fairly consistent bedtime—nothing rigid, just generally the same window—lets your internal clock learn what to expect.\n\nThink of it less like a strict routine and more like guiding yourself gradually into quiet mode. Even one or two small habits done consistently can make falling asleep noticeably easier.\nUser turn 3: I like the idea of a consistent wind-down window—I'm pretty much always asleep by 10:30 PM. If I want to keep that bedtime, when would you suggest starting the wind-down, and what would a simple 20–30 minute routine look like that doesn’t involve screens?\nAssistant turn 4: If you’re usually asleep by 10:30, easing into wind‑down mode around 10:00 is a good fit. You don’t need anything elaborate; just a short, repeatable sequence that tells your brain, “we’re powering down.”\n\nSomething like this can work: around ten, switch off bright lights and move to softer lamps. Give yourself a couple of minutes to tidy your space or set out anything you’ll want for the morning so your mind isn’t spinning with little tasks. After that, do something that gently shifts your body into rest—maybe a warm shower, a slow stretch, or even just sitting quietly with a cup of water or tea and breathing a bit more deliberately. Finish with a calm activity you enjoy, like reading a few pages of a book or jotting down a quick note about the day.\n\nThe goal isn’t to fill the whole half hour with tasks; it’s to create a predictable, calm slope into sleep so that by the time you’re in bed, your body is already halfway there.\nUser turn 5: That sounds doable. Can you give me 2–3 different 20–30 minute wind-down “templates” I could rotate between (one for when I feel wired/anxious, one for normal nights, and one for when I’m physically tense), all starting around 10:00 and ending with lights out by 10:30?\nAssistant turn 6: Here are three simple templates you can swap between, each still keeping things calm and conversational in tone.\n\nFor wired or anxious evenings, start around ten by dimming the lights and spending a minute or two doing something grounding, like putting away a couple of items or getting your space settled. Then sit somewhere comfortable and do a slow breathing pattern for a few minutes—nothing fancy, just longer exhales than inhales. After that, journal briefly to unload whatever your mind is carrying. Wrap up with a few quiet pages of a low‑intensity book, then head to bed around 10:30.\n\nFor an ordinary night, keep it very simple. Around ten, switch to softer light, brush your teeth, change into sleep clothes, and do a quick stretch or just walk slowly around your place to signal the shift. End with something soothing but light, like reading, listening to calm music, or sipping warm tea. Lights out at 10:30.\n\nFor physically tense nights, start at ten with a warm shower or even just a warm face wash and neck rinse to loosen your muscles. Follow that with ten minutes of gentle stretching or using a massage ball on tight areas. After your body feels a bit more open, settle into bed or a chair with a calming activity like reading or a brief body‑scan meditation (done without a screen). When you feel that heaviness start to build, turn the lights out at 10:30.\n\nYou can rotate these depending on how the day felt, and they’ll all still line up with your sleep window.\nUser turn 7: Could you write those three templates out as a minute-by-minute schedule from 10:00 to 10:30 (including what time to get into bed), and suggest a couple of non-screen options for the “quiet activity” part that won’t accidentally keep me up?\nAssistant turn 8: Here’s how each routine can look laid out from 10:00 to 10:30, written as a simple flow rather than a list.\n\nFor wired or anxious nights, begin at 10:00 by dimming the lights and taking just a minute or two to settle your space. By about 10:05, sit somewhere comfortable and spend five minutes breathing slowly with longer exhales. Around 10:10, shift into a brief journaling session until roughly 10:20. At that point, get into bed and spend the next ten minutes doing a quiet, calming activity that won’t ramp you up, like reading something gentle or listening to soft, non‑narrative music. Lights go out at 10:30.\n\nFor a normal night, ease in at 10:00 by switching to soft lighting and moving through your bathroom routine. That will take you to around 10:10, when you can stretch lightly or just move slowly to signal the transition into rest. By 10:15, get into bed and let the remaining fifteen minutes be a wind‑down with something quiet, like leafing through a mellow book or listening to a simple ambient soundscape. Turn the lights off at 10:30.\n\nFor a physically tense night, start at 10:00 with a warm shower or warm rinse that lasts about ten minutes. By 10:10, move into gentle stretching or light self‑massage until around 10:20. Then get into bed and spend the last ten minutes with a soothing, low‑engagement activity such as a short body‑scan done silently or reading a familiar book you don’t feel tempted to stay up for. Lights out at 10:30.\n\nFor the quiet activity portion in any of these, good options are reading something calm and not plot‑heavy, listening to soft instrumental music, doing a simple body‑scan in your head, or even holding a warm mug of herbal tea while you unwind. All of these tend to wind you down rather than wake you up.\n\n=== Session 45 [Time: 2025-09-18 05:23] ===\nUser turn 1: I'm trying to plan my Sunday schedule for this week. Can you remind me what time the NFL games usually start?\nAssistant turn 2: NFL games typically have a standard schedule, but it can vary depending on the week and the teams playing. Here's a general outline of when NFL games usually start:\n\n* Early Games: 1:00 PM ET (10:00 AM PT)\n* Late Afternoon Games: 4:05 PM ET (1:05 PM PT) or 4:25 PM ET (1:25 PM PT)\n* Sunday Night Football: 8:20 PM ET (5:20 PM PT)\n* Monday Night Football (not on Sundays, but I'll mention it for completeness): 8:15 PM ET (5:15 PM PT)\n\nKeep in mind that these times are subject to change, especially if there are international games or special events. You can always check the official NFL website or your local TV listings for the exact schedule and times for this week's games.\n\nHope this helps you plan your Sunday schedule!\nUser turn 3: By the way, I'm planning to watch the games with friends at our favorite sports bar. Speaking of which, do you know what time they open on Sundays? I seem to recall getting there pretty early last week, and I want to make sure I don't miss the kickoff.\nAssistant turn 4: I'm happy to help! However, I'm a large language model, I don't have access to specific information about your local sports bar's hours of operation. Those hours can vary depending on the establishment and their policies.\n\nI'd recommend giving your sports bar a quick call or checking their website or social media pages to confirm their Sunday hours. You can also ask about their opening time, any special deals or promotions they might have for the games, and whether they have any reserved seating or tables available.\n\nIt's always a good idea to plan ahead and confirm the details to ensure you and your friends have a great time watching the games!\nUser turn 5: I'll just check last week's schedule real quick to see what time we got there. Ah yeah, we got to the sports bar at 10 am, perfect timing to grab some good seats and get settled in before the games started.\nAssistant turn 6: It sounds like you're all set then! 10 am is a great time to get to the sports bar, especially if you want to grab good seats and get settled in before the games start. Just keep in mind that the sports bar's hours might still be subject to change, so it's always a good idea to double-check with them before heading out.\n\nAlso, don't forget to plan for food and drinks, and make sure you and your friends are all on the same page about how you're going to watch the games. Are you going to focus on one game, or try to catch multiple games at once? Either way, it sounds like you're in for a fun day of football and friendship!\nUser turn 7: What's the lineup for this week's NFL games? Are there any particularly interesting matchups I should be looking out for?\nAssistant turn 8: I'm glad you're excited about the games! However, as a large language model, I don't have real-time access to the current NFL schedule or specific game matchups. The NFL schedule can change from week to week, and I don't want to provide outdated or incorrect information.\n\nBut I can suggest some ways for you to find out the latest information on this week's NFL games! You can:\n\n1. Check the official NFL website (nfl.com) or the NFL app for the latest schedule and game information.\n2. Follow your favorite teams or sports news websites on social media to stay up-to-date on the latest news and schedules.\n3. Tune in to sports news channels or sports radio stations for expert analysis and updates on the upcoming games.\n4. Check your local TV listings or sports bar's schedule to see which games will be broadcast in your area.\n\nAs for interesting matchups, I can suggest some general tips to look out for:\n\n* Rivalry games: Look for games between teams with a long history of competition or geographic rivalries, as these tend to be intense and exciting matchups.\n* Top-ranked teams: Keep an eye on games featuring top-ranked teams or teams with strong winning streaks, as these can be thrilling to watch.\n* Upset potential: Look for games where an underdog team has a good chance of pulling off an upset, as these can be exciting and unpredictable.\n\nI hope this helps, and I'm sure you'll have a great time watching the games with your friends!\nUser turn 9: Can you give me some recommendations on where to find good sports news and analysis? I'm looking for some new sources to follow for my favorite teams and sports.\nAssistant turn 10: Here are some popular and reputable sources for sports news and analysis:\n\n**Websites:**\n\n1. **ESPN** (espn.com): One of the most well-known and comprehensive sports websites, with news, analysis, and commentary on various sports.\n2. **NFL.com** (nfl.com): The official website of the National Football League, with news, scores, schedules, and analysis.\n3. **CBS Sports** (cbssports.com): A popular website with news, scores, and analysis on various sports, including the NFL, NBA, MLB, and more.\n4. **Yahoo! Sports** (sports.yahoo.com): A well-established website with news, scores, and analysis on various sports.\n5. **The Athletic** (theathletic.com): A subscription-based website with in-depth, ad-free coverage of various sports, including NFL, NBA, MLB, and more.\n6. **Bleacher Report** (bleacherreport.com): A popular website with news, analysis, and commentary on various sports.\n\n**Social Media:**\n\n1. **Twitter**: Follow your favorite teams, players, and sports journalists for real-time updates and analysis.\n2. **Instagram**: Many sports teams and journalists have Instagram accounts with behind-the-scenes content and analysis.\n\n**Podcasts:**\n\n1. **The Ringer NFL Show** (theringer.com/nfl): A popular podcast with in-depth analysis and discussion on the NFL.\n2. **Pardon My Take** (barstoolsports.com/pardon-my-take): A humorous and entertaining podcast covering various sports.\n3. **The Dan Le Batard Show with Stugotz** (espn.com/podcenter/dan-le-batard): A popular podcast with news, analysis, and commentary on various sports.\n\n**TV Channels:**\n\n1. **ESPN**: A 24/7 sports network with various shows, including SportsCenter, NFL Live, and more.\n2. **NFL Network**: A 24/7 network dedicated to the NFL, with news, analysis, and live games.\n3. **Fox Sports**: A network with various sports programming, including NFL, MLB, and college football coverage.\n\n**Local Sources:**\n\n1. **Local newspapers**: Follow local newspapers (e.g., The New York Times, Chicago Tribune) for coverage of your local teams.\n2. **Team websites**: Visit your favorite team's official website for news, updates, and analysis.\n\nRemember to diversify your sources to get a well-rounded perspective on your favorite teams and sports. Enjoy!\nUser turn 11: I'm thinking of following the Premier League more closely this season. Do you know which teams are currently at the top of the table? And how's Liverpool doing so far?\nAssistant turn 12: The Premier League is an exciting league to follow!\n\nAs a large language model, I don't have real-time access to current sports information, including the latest Premier League standings. The league table can change rapidly with each matchday, and I don't want to provide outdated information.\n\nHowever, I can suggest some ways for you to stay up-to-date on the Premier League:\n\n1. **Official Premier League website**: Visit the official Premier League website (premierleague.com) for the latest news, scores, and standings.\n2. **Sports news websites**: Check sports news websites like BBC Sport, Sky Sports, or ESPN for the latest Premier League news and updates.\n3. **Social media**: Follow the Premier League, teams, and players on social media platforms like Twitter, Instagram, or Facebook to stay informed.\n4. **Mobile apps**: Download mobile apps like the Premier League app, BBC Sport app, or ESPN app to get real-time scores, news, and updates.\n\nAs for Liverpool, they're a fantastic team with a rich history in the Premier League! You can check their official website (liverpoolfc.com) or follow them on social media to stay updated on their performance this season.\n\nRemember, the Premier League season is long and unpredictable, with many twists and turns. It's exciting to follow a team throughout the season, and I'm sure you'll enjoy watching Liverpool (or any other team you choose to support)!\n\n=== Session 10 [Time: 2025-06-25 04:21] ===\nUser turn 1: I'm looking to schedule a weekly review of my tasks and goals. Can you help me set a reminder for that? Also, just a heads up, I'm not available on Tuesdays and Thursdays from 7:00 pm to 8:00 pm, so maybe we can schedule it around that time.\nAssistant turn 2: I'd be happy to help you set a reminder for a weekly review of your tasks and goals.\n\nSince you're not available on Tuesdays and Thursdays from 7:00 pm to 8:00 pm, let's explore other options. Here are a few suggestions:\n\n1. **Monday morning**: Start your week off on the right foot by reviewing your tasks and goals on Monday morning. This can help you set a clear plan for the week ahead.\n2. **Wednesday lunch break**: Use your lunch break on Wednesday to review your progress, adjust your schedule as needed, and refocus on your goals.\n3. **Friday afternoon**: Take some time on Friday afternoon to review your week, celebrate your accomplishments, and prepare for the weekend.\n4. **Sunday evening**: Set aside some time on Sunday evening to reflect on your week, plan for the upcoming week, and make any necessary adjustments.\n\nWhich of these options works best for you? Or do you have a different time in mind?\n\nOnce we settle on a time, I can help you set a reminder to ensure you don't forget your weekly review.\nUser turn 